jotain NEWS                                               -*- outline -*-

User-visible changes worth mentioning, newest first, after the GNU
Coding Standards (NEWS File).  jotain has no tagged releases yet, so
each version stands for one era of the history and ends at the matching
era/* git tag; CHANGELOG.md lists the changes in detail.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.9.0 (2026-11-19) [era 9: port back]

** New features

  The work of 0.8.1 is part of this repository again: pull request
  review, Kotlin, Astro, the new language modes and the merge tools.

  Per-user installs: services.jotain.users.<name> in the NixOS module
  installs the wrapped Emacs, the EDITOR and VISUAL scripts and jotctl
  in a user's profile.

  Dev shell: nix develop (or nix-shell) provides the tooling for this
  repository, including prek, convco and gitleaks for the commit hooks.

** Changes in behavior

  Emacs 31.1 is the oldest supported version.

  The LikeC4 major mode is jotain-likec4-mode.

  Commits follow Conventional Commits, checked by a convco commit-msg
  hook, with gitleaks scanning staged files.

** Removed features

  Darwin, aarch64-linux and the terminal-only build were dropped: the
  flake builds one pgtk Emacs for x86_64-linux.

  The nix-on-droid module, devenv and services.jotain.devenv.enable
  were removed.

** Bug fixes

  services.jotain.spell.dictionaries reaches jinx again: a non-empty
  list sets ASPELL_CONF in the wrapped Emacs.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.8.1 (2026-11-19) [side branch: monorepo]

  The configuration was developed inside a monorepo from 2026-10-11 to
  2026-11-19.  Changes to the other projects there are not listed.

** New features

  Repository and pull request review: repo-review shows the files of a
  repository as a queue of read-only diffs, and C-x p V
  (repo-review-pullreq) opens a Forge pull request or merge request.
  Draft comments are saved with c and posted with P after a
  confirmation, files are marked viewed with v, and optional gptel
  commands draft and check review comments.

  Kotlin: Eglot starts kotlin-lsp or kotlin-language-server, ktfmt
  formats on save, ktlint and detekt report through Flymake, and dape
  debugs through kotlin-debug-adapter.  C-c C-t runs Gradle tests and
  C-c C-z opens a REPL.  All tools come from the project PATH.

  More language modes: Astro, KDL, jq, GLSL, Gherkin, MDX, JSONC and
  build.zig.zon files.  Man pages get Flymake diagnostics from mandoc.

  Merge tools: C-c ^ S in smerge-mode re-resolves a conflicted file with
  mergiraf, and jotain-magit-delta-enable renders magit diffs through
  delta.

  devenv-shell activates a devenv environment for one buffer without
  trusting the project.

** Changes in behavior

  The configuration is Linux-only and requires Emacs 31.1.  The
  newcomers-presets theme is enabled, and settings equal to their
  defaults are gone.

  Fonts follow the design system: Fira Sans for body text and IBM Plex
  Mono for code.

  gptel defaults to the direct Claude backend (claude-sonnet-5-5,
  ANTHROPIC_API_KEY).  The OpenRouter backend stays selectable from the
  C-c S menu.

  Open .h files as C, format Python with ruff and use lua-ts-mode for
  Lua.

** Bug fixes

  .scss files open in scss-mode, exec-path-from-shell imports the login
  shell PATH, and a prompt opened without a frame no longer locks the
  keyboard of the daemon.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.8.0 (2026-10-09) [era 8: current generation]

** New features

  The current shape of jotain: early-init.el, a small init.el and one
  lisp/init-*.el module per concern, on an Emacs built by emacs.nix
  whose default builds come from the binary cache.  The distribution
  bundles every package and every tree-sitter grammar.

  Modules: the Home Manager module runs Jotain as a user daemon with
  emacsclient, a byte-compiled config and the jotctl command.  NixOS,
  nix-darwin and nix-on-droid modules install the same distribution.

  Documentation: the jotain.info manual (C-h i d m Jotain RET), a
  module option reference, a generated package reference, a keybindings
  page, an Emacs Lisp API reference and a website on GitHub Pages.

  devenv integration: devenv.el loads a project's devenv.sh
  environment, with allow and revoke trust and a mode-line status, and
  uses the language servers the project provides.

  Languages: Go, Zig, Bazel, Meson, OCaml, Haskell, Just, QML, Robot
  Framework, Structurizr, LikeC4, CUDA, and C and C++ on tree-sitter.
  A language-support matrix reports what works per language.

  Version control: magit, Jujutsu (jj) support, git-stats counters in
  the modeline and a quick jump between changed files.

  AI: eca, tagref and gptel with OpenRouter models.

  Also new: Tempel snippets, vertico in a posframe, jinx spell checking
  with bundled dictionaries, Org Babel as a notebook, an emoji picker,
  a docker TUI, a Ghostty-based terminal through ghostel and the Jylhis
  design system themes.

** Changes in behavior

  Emacs 31 is the baseline, and the Linux GUI build is pgtk.

  Startup takes about 3 s instead of 10 s, and package archives are
  never fetched at startup.

  TAB indents and completes, RET inserts a newline and C-M-i completes.

  Primary entry points moved into the user-reserved C-c key space.

  treesit-auto and envrc were replaced by major-mode-remap-alist and
  the native devenv integration.

  TLS certificates are verified through GnuTLS and the Network Security
  Manager, and Org Babel asks before it evaluates code.

** Removed features

  wakatime and activity-watch tracking, the jylhis Emacs fork and the
  legacy GUI variants.

** Bug fixes

  Opening a file is no longer eight times slower with tree-sitter mode
  selection, and the daemon no longer crashes at startup on a void
  vc-git-root.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.7.0 (2026-04-07) [era 7: the rewrite on next]

  A new configuration started from vanilla Emacs on the next branch: a
  minimal early-init.el and init.el, Emacs built from source by
  emacs.nix with every tree-sitter grammar, a devenv shell, the first
  ERT tests and Mintlify docs.  A PostHog telemetry module was added
  and disabled again within the week.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.6.0 (2026-04-01) [era 6: first generation]

** New features

  The configuration became its own repository and flake, named jotain
  on 2025-10-20, with a Home Manager module, overlays, CI and tests.

  Package dependencies are extracted from use-package forms, and all
  tree-sitter grammars are installed.

  New packages and modes: cape, kind-icon, kkp, Gited, csv-mode,
  jinja2-mode, pdf-tools, Vue and React support, the Enlight dashboard
  and the claude-code-ide integration.

  macOS is supported through darwin-emacs.

** Changes in behavior

  Emacs 30.2 is the oldest supported version.

  The theme follows the system setting; dark mode uses doom-nord.

  The default fill-column is 100, and yes-or-no prompts no longer use
  dialog boxes.

** Removed features

  Obsidian support, bash-language-server and the devJot CLI.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.5.0 (2025-09-28) [era 5: NixOS repository]

  The configuration was deployed with Home Manager from the owner's
  NixOS repository.  It was a literate init.org for a month and then a
  modular init.el with config/*.el files.  It gained Eglot, AI tools
  (aider, Copilot, Claude), a platform support system, an application
  launcher, 1Password as an auth-source and Obsidian notes.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.4.0 (2024-07-20) [era 4: standalone emacs.d]

  A new ~/.emacs.d from scratch with straight.el, LSP and DAP,
  tree-sitter, treemacs and one personal/setup-*.el file per topic.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.3.0 (2017-08-14) [era 3: own config again]

  The fork was replaced by a single init.el; GLSL support was added.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.2.0 (2017-03-31) [era 2: upstream fork]

  The config became a fork of Purcell's emacs.d with the old settings
  merged in.

* Noteworthy changes in 0.1.0 (2017-03-30) [era 1: dotfiles]

  The first init.el, kept with the dotfiles from 2016-07-10: evil-mode,
  rtags and clang-format for C and C++, Python completion and linting,
  Erlang and web-mode.
